1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:01,625
Knight. Knife. Knee. Know.

2
00:00:01,655 --> 00:00:04,471
Every one of them starts
with a letter you never say.

3
00:00:04,498 --> 00:00:05,556
So why is it there?

4
00:00:06,733 --> 00:00:08,065
Here is the surprise.

5
00:00:08,124 --> 00:00:13,117
A thousand years ago, English speakers
said that K out loud, every single time.

6
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:19,259
In Old English, the word was spelled c,
n, i, h, t, and it sounded like k-nicht.

7
00:00:19,310 --> 00:00:21,253
It did not even mean a warrior.

8
00:00:21,304 --> 00:00:23,145
It meant a boy, or a servant.

9
00:00:24,333 --> 00:00:25,917
German still does this today.

10
00:00:25,954 --> 00:00:28,762
The German word for knee
is Knie, with a clear K.

11
00:00:28,780 --> 00:00:32,398
And the German word Knecht, a
servant, is a cousin of our knight.

12
00:00:33,600 --> 00:00:34,914
So what happened?

13
00:00:34,987 --> 00:00:40,567
In 1476, William Caxton set up the
first printing press in England, and

14
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:43,182
printed spellings began to settle.

15
00:00:43,242 --> 00:00:48,697
Then, over about a century, speakers
slowly stopped saying K before N.

16
00:00:48,723 --> 00:00:51,516
By around 1750, the sound was gone.

17
00:00:52,700 --> 00:00:56,905
The sound changed, but the spelling had
already been printed in thousands of

18
00:00:56,924 --> 00:01:00,876
books. So the letter stayed on the
page, long after it left the mouth.

19
00:01:02,067 --> 00:01:03,303
So here is your rule.

20
00:01:03,336 --> 00:01:06,020
When a word starts with
K, N, the K is silent.

21
00:01:06,062 --> 00:01:07,604
Knock, knot, knit, knowledge.

22
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:09,352
You are not reading a mistake.

23
00:01:09,399 --> 00:01:10,814
You are reading a fossil.

